{
  "MANIFEST_VERSION": 2,
  "_comment": "Every symlink-source path in this repo, and where init_harness.py should link it in a consumer project. Each entry's \"dest\" is resolved against a base directory chosen by \"dest_root\": \"repo\" (default when absent) resolves against the consumer repo root, \"active\" resolves against .friday/active/ — used for every generated harness/... path now that the harness's generated output lives inside the submodule instead of at the consumer root. Each entry's \"src\" is resolved against a base directory chosen by \"src_root\": \"templates\" (default when absent) resolves against templates/ in this repo, \"submodule\" resolves against this repo's own root — used for the one file (USER_GUIDE.md) that deliberately lives at the submodule root rather than under templates/. Adding a new symlink-source file to this template only requires an entry here, not a change to init_harness.py. Each entry may also carry an \"owner\" key: \"harness\" (the default when absent) means the file is derived tooling/state the harness regenerates and is therefore excluded from the consumer repo's git history via .git/info/exclude — see compute_excluded_paths() in init_harness.py; \"project\" means the PROJECT authors/owns this file (README, AGENTS.md, docs/RESULTS.md, the Docker build inputs, ...) and it is always committed regardless. Every symlinks entry is \"harness\" by construction — a link straight into the .friday/ submodule is never hand-edited in the consumer repo — so \"owner\" only ever needs to be written explicitly on a handful of repo-rooted materialize entries. A per-entry \"tracker\" key (\"none\" or \"configured\") additionally gates an entry on whether this project's TRACKER_KIND is \"none\" or something else — see status_history.md's two entries below, the only place this currently matters.",
  "legacy_dests": {
    "_comment": "Snapshot of every current 'harness/…' dest (from both symlinks and materialize below) as of the last version before v0.13.0 relocates the whole harness/ tree into .friday/active/harness/. --untrack-harness works by intersecting `git ls-files` with the CURRENT manifest's dests — once v0.13.0 lands, no dest anywhere in this file has a 'harness/…' prefix any more, so that intersection goes empty and a project that adopted HARNESS_TRACKING pre-move gets 'Nothing to untrack' instead of the migration it's asking for. legacy_dests is the fallback list --untrack-legacy intersects with `git ls-files` instead, so the move-off-root step keeps working for every project that committed harness/** before upgrading past 'since'. Deliberately does NOT include .claude/ or .agents/ dests — those stay at the consumer repo root even after v0.13.0 (only harness/** moves), so they remain reachable through the ordinary --untrack-harness path and don't belong in a list of paths that are going away.",
